Iterator
---------

Iterator is an object used to traverse (visit) elements one by one from a collection.

Some collections - HashSet, TreeSet, Queue don't support indexing.
Iterator works with ALL collections. That's why Iterator is preferred than for loops

If we use for loop for modifying collection while looping it throws ConcurrentModificationException.
Whereas Iterator removes elements safely while modifying the collection while iteration.

Methods:
    hasNext() - Checks if another element exists
    next() - returns the next element
    remove() - removes the current element safely

Example: To remove inactive users from collection of users

-----------------

Iterator is an interface in Java used to traverse elements of a collection one by one.
It provides a common way to iterate through collections such as List, Set, and Queue without
depending on their internal implementation.

Iterator is used to traverse collections in a uniform way. It is especially useful for
collections like HashSet that do not support indexing. It also allows elements to be removed
safely while iterating.

Difference between a for loop and an Iterator
----------------------------------------------

            For Loop	                                     Iterator
Best for arrays and indexed collections	            Works with all collection types

Uses indexes	                                    Uses hasNext() and next()

Removing elements while iterating may               Supports safe removal using remove()
cause ConcurrentModificationException



